WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.960 --> 00:00:04.900
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:05.400
Good day.

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:06.800
I'm Dean Marchiori.

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:09.020
I'm a statistician
from Sydney, Australia.

00:00:09.020 --> 00:00:11.300
And I'm interested in
reproducible analysis

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:14.750
and workflow choices in R.
But in my day-to-day practice

00:00:14.750 --> 00:00:17.420
in industry as a
commercial data scientist,

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:20.210
is my work actually
reproducible?

00:00:20.210 --> 00:00:22.550
To answer this, I
conducted a reproducibility

00:00:22.550 --> 00:00:26.240
retrospective or Repro-Retro.

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:28.640
I went back through the last
year of my commercial data

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:31.040
science projects and
rated at each project

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:34.430
across a number of dimensions
that are valued in my practice.

00:00:34.430 --> 00:00:37.280
You can pick aspects that suit
your work but the dimensions I

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:42.350
picked cover if an analysis were
replicable, modular, auditable,

00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:44.570
automated and collaborative.

00:00:44.570 --> 00:00:47.270
So what were the results?

00:00:47.270 --> 00:00:51.650
Out of 55 projects, I scored
an average of 3.8 out of 5.

00:00:51.650 --> 00:00:53.910
This is good, but not great.

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:55.970
So if I care so
much about this, why

00:00:55.970 --> 00:00:57.890
wasn't my work rated higher?

00:00:57.890 --> 00:01:01.790
Here are the lessons I
took from my Repro-Retro.

00:01:01.790 --> 00:01:04.040
Controversial opinion
number 1-- not everything

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:07.340
needs to be reproducible,
while all projects benefited

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:10.160
from some aspect of
reproducibility, the most

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:13.640
important consideration for
me was "How do you tell, what

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:17.310
needs to be reproducible?"

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:19.190
Enter my Fiji test.

00:01:19.190 --> 00:01:21.750
So if you have a task that
sufficiently important,

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:24.170
you may want to
consider the Fiji test.

00:01:24.170 --> 00:01:28.310
Aussies like Fiji because
it's only a few hours away.

00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:31.250
But if you're lying on a
beach in holidays in Fiji just

00:01:31.250 --> 00:01:34.010
spare a thought for your
colleagues back in the office.

00:01:34.010 --> 00:01:36.290
If they need to rerun
some of your analysis,

00:01:36.290 --> 00:01:39.530
will they be able to find
it, understand your work,

00:01:39.530 --> 00:01:43.460
run it without too much fuss
and make reasonable enhancements

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:44.973
or small changes.

00:01:44.973 --> 00:01:47.390
The last thing you want while
you're sipping a piña colada

00:01:47.390 --> 00:01:50.210
is to be disturbed by a weird
phone call about some model

00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:52.580
that you trained.

00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:55.400
When I looked at the type of
analytics task I completed,

00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:57.990
not everything needed
to pass the Fiji test.

00:01:57.990 --> 00:02:01.540
A lot of work in industry
is ad hoc and quite simple.

00:02:01.540 --> 00:02:04.340
In a commercial or a high
pressure environment,

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:05.810
I think it's
perfectly reasonable

00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:08.870
to have different standards
on workflow choices

00:02:08.870 --> 00:02:10.880
depending on the type
of work and the return

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:12.680
on investment that you need.

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:17.120
So long as a reasonable standard
is maintained and as long as

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:19.980
your team is consistent
with these choices.

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:21.800
However, this may
mean getting better

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:24.290
at anticipating the full
scope of the project

00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:26.750
before rushing into
solving a problem, which

00:02:26.750 --> 00:02:28.418
can be a challenge.

00:02:28.418 --> 00:02:29.960
In addition, it
gives you the freedom

00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:32.120
to pick a more lightweight
and flexible workflow

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:34.610
choice if desired.

00:02:34.610 --> 00:02:37.460
My next lesson was picking
the right tools for the job.

00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:39.710
You know, I've been searching
for the biggest and best

00:02:39.710 --> 00:02:43.550
workflow choice but I found
it paid to have options.

00:02:43.550 --> 00:02:46.100
When I examined my tooling
in workflow options,

00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:48.680
the results of my
Repro-Retro varied.

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:51.020
Using a basic directory
structure template

00:02:51.020 --> 00:02:53.990
to organize my projects
was OK around it.

00:02:53.990 --> 00:02:55.820
Quickly got cumbersome.

00:02:55.820 --> 00:02:59.060
Notebooks like R Markdown
were nicely replicable

00:02:59.060 --> 00:03:02.360
but tended to be a bit of
a dumping ground for code.

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:04.970
Drake, a more formal
workflow system

00:03:04.970 --> 00:03:07.010
carried a bit more
overhead and more

00:03:07.010 --> 00:03:09.830
admin to get used to
but overall it enforced

00:03:09.830 --> 00:03:12.200
much better standards,
particularly around writing

00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:14.510
functional and modular code.

00:03:14.510 --> 00:03:17.540
While any system can be made
arbitrarily sophisticated

00:03:17.540 --> 00:03:19.460
and do a good job,
I found it more

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:21.440
effective to have a
few different workflow

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:24.200
choices available rather
than try a one size fits

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:25.550
all approach.

00:03:25.550 --> 00:03:27.260
All workflow choices
are naturally

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:29.510
optimized for
certain applications

00:03:29.510 --> 00:03:31.100
and I found it
better to only take

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:35.450
on additional complexity,
when the job really needed it.

00:03:35.450 --> 00:03:38.930
Next I found in my work, I had a
tendency to mistake replication

00:03:38.930 --> 00:03:42.980
for reproduction and the
same goes for automation.

00:03:42.980 --> 00:03:46.020
Getting your code to rerun
is only part of the solution.

00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:48.920
So consider the factors
that matter in your practice

00:03:48.920 --> 00:03:51.530
and be deliberate about
what you optimize for

00:03:51.530 --> 00:03:54.350
and use that to guide
your workflow choice.

00:03:54.350 --> 00:03:56.550
And how do you do that?

00:03:56.550 --> 00:03:58.550
Well, do a Repro-Retro.

00:03:58.550 --> 00:04:01.010
The process wasn't
and isn't intended

00:04:01.010 --> 00:04:03.200
to be scientific or unbiased.

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:06.440
The true value of a Repro-Retro
is in the self-reflection

00:04:06.440 --> 00:04:07.710
and improvement.

00:04:07.710 --> 00:04:09.650
So go and dig
through your old code

00:04:09.650 --> 00:04:12.907
and think about how you
might do things better.

00:04:12.907 --> 00:04:14.990
If you want to learn more,
you can reach out to me

00:04:14.990 --> 00:04:18.023
or visit my analysis
flow GitHub repo.

00:04:18.023 --> 00:04:20.690
I would also like to acknowledge
and thank my collaborator Miles

00:04:20.690 --> 00:04:24.300
McBain for many interesting
discussions in this space.

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:26.900
Thank you and good luck.

00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:29.050
[MUSIC PLAYING]

